      section: "CS",
      q: "The synchronous sequential circuit below, built using JK flip-flops, is initialized with Q₂Q₁Q₀ = 000. Q₂ and Q₁ are wired directly in sequence (Q₂ drives J₁, Q̄₂ drives K₁; Q₁ drives J₀, Q̄₁ drives K₀), while Q₀'s outputs are fed back around to the first flip-flop (Q₀ drives J₂, Q̄₀ drives K₂), forming a 3-bit Johnson (twisted-ring) counter. The state sequence for this circuit for the next 3 clock cycles is",
      figure: "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 180\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:420px;height:auto\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"50\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"50\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\"/><rect x=\"300\" y=\"50\" width=\"80\" height=\"60\"/></g><g font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"48\" y=\"68\">J</text><text x=\"48\" y=\"100\">K</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"68\">Q2</text><text x=\"86\" y=\"100\">Q̄2</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"68\">J</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"100\">K</text><text x=\"222\" y=\"68\">Q1</text><text x=\"216\" y=\"100\">Q̄1</text><text x=\"308\" y=\"68\">J</text><text x=\"308\" y=\"100\">K</text><text x=\"352\" y=\"68\">Q0</text><text x=\"346\" y=\"100\">Q̄0</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"84\">C→</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"84\">C→</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"84\">C→</text></g><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"63\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"63\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"97\"/><line x1=\"250\" y1=\"63\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"63\"/><line x1=\"250\" y1=\"97\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"97\"/><path d=\"M380,63 L400,63 L400,20 L20,20 L20,55\" /><path d=\"M380,97 L410,97 L410,140 L20,140 L20,105\" /></g><g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"140\" y=\"15\">Q0 feedback to J2 (top rail)</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"155\">Q̄0 feedback to K2 (bottom rail)</text></g></svg>",
      options: ["001, 010, 011","111, 110, 101","100, 110, 111","100, 011, 001"],
      answer: 2,
      marks: 2,
      explanation: "1. This circuit is a 3-BIT JOHNSON (twisted-ring) COUNTER: each flip-flop passes its OWN current value directly to the NEXT flip-flop in sequence (Q2→ next stage, Q1→ next stage), except the LAST stage's output is fed back INVERTED into the FIRST flip-flop's input — this \"twist\" (feeding back the complement) is what distinguishes a Johnson counter from an ordinary shift register/ring counter. 2. Starting at Q2Q1Q0=000: on the first clock edge, Q0's current value (0) shifts into becoming Q1's contribution, Q1's current value (0) shifts toward Q2, and Q0's OWN new value comes from the INVERTED feedback of the previous Q0 (0 inverted = 1) — giving the new state 100. 3. From 100: shifting again with the same twisted feedback rule gives 110. 4. From 110: shifting again gives 111. 5. The sequence for the next 3 clock cycles is 100, 110, 111, matching option C."
